WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.640
Let’s say you really need
to find reliable information

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.840
about the best diet for
high blood pressure,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.000
or heart disease,
or diabetes.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:11.360
Where do you go?

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.920
Do you go to a website
sponsored by Big Pharma

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.720
that wants to sell you
pills to fix your problem?

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.760
Or, do you want
to treat the cause?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.280
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.800
with the latest peer-
reviewed research

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.840
on the best ways to eat
healthy and live longer.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.160
Today we turn a laser-like focus to
that round tuber we call the potato.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.720
Are they good for us?
Bad for us?

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.400
Here’s our first story.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.520
The trouble for white
potatoes began in 2006,

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.520
when the Harvard Nurses’ Health
Study, which had followed

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.200
the diets and diseases of tens
of thousands of women for 20 years,

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.000
found that greater potato
intake was associated with

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.400
a greater likelihood of
getting type 2 diabetes.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.640
Okay, but wait. Of the hundred
or so pounds of potatoes

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.680
Americans eat every year,
most are in the deep-fried form

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.360
of potato chips or French fries.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.520
What happened when they looked
specifically at mashed or baked potatoes?

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.560
They found the same link with diabetes.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.240
Okay, but what might
potato eaters eat more of?

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.520
I’ll give you a hint by rephrasing that as:

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.120
what might meat-and-potatoes
people eat more of?

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.520
Indeed, people who ate
more potatoes ate more meat,

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.680
and we know that animal
protein on its own

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.320
is associated with
increased diabetes risk.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.640
But the researchers tried
to statistically adjust

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.920
for that and still found
increased risk with potatoes.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.240
Well, what do people put
on baked and mashed potatoes?

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.440
Butter and sour cream. Again,
the researchers tried to adjust

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.560
for other dietary factors
like these as well as effectively

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.120
looking at the ratio
between plant and animal fats,

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.120
and whether potato
eaters drank more soda,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.840
or maybe skimped on other
vegetables, and still

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.800
there seemed to be this
potato diabetes association.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.840
Okay, but that was just one study.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.360
By 2015, Harvard researchers had
also looked into other cohorts,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.320
including the all-male Health
Professionals Follow-up Study

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.040
to complement the all-
female Nurse’s studies,

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.880
and they continued to find a
small increased diabetes risk

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.520
associated with baked,
boiled, or mashed potatoes,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.040
though French fries do indeed
appear nearly five times worse.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:23.040
The authors concluded that
potatoes are considered to be

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.440
a healthy vegetable
in dietary guidelines;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.520
however, the current ﬁndings cast
serious doubts on that classiﬁcation.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.040
Walt Willet, the chair of Harvard’s
nutrition department at the time,

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.200
went a step further, suggesting potatoes

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.600
should be siloed up there with candy.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.600
A meta-analysis of potato consumption
and risk of type 2 diabetes

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.120
published in 2018 combined all
six of the prospective studies

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.720
that has been done to date, and they
found about a 20 percent increase

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.240
in diabetes risk associated with
each serving of potatoes a day,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.720
concluding long-term high
consumption of potato may be

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.200
strongly associated with
increased risk of diabetes.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.120
But, again, the great majority of
this potato consumption was fried,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.360
and we know there are all
sorts of nasty things like

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.680
advanced glycation end
products in deep fried foods.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.160
The researchers weren’t able to assess

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.040
French fries versus non-fried potatoes.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.400
Even just three servings of
fries a week is associated

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.440
with nearly 20 percent greater
risk of type 2 diabetes,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.000
whereas there was only a
tiny associated risk with

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.200
potatoes in general, and that
included the fries mixed in.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.880
The world’s largest manufacturer
of frozen French fries

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.880
took issue with this conclusion,

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.480
laying claim to one in three
fries eaten on planet Earth

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.440
to the tune of billions of
dollars, they have the money

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.400
to fund reviews like this one
to cast doubt on the science.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.560
But they do have an actual point.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.280
Observational studies can
never prove cause-and-effect,

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.800
and maybe potato consumption,
even baked potato consumption,

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.920
may just be a marker for an
unhealthy diet in general.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:55.280
As much as researchers try to
adjust for these other factors,

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:59.600
as the journal of the Potato Association
of America is quick to remind us,

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.840
it’s not possible to completely
separate the effects

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.520
of potatoes and French
fries from the effects

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.720
of the overall crappy
Standard American Diet.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.760
If only there was a country
where potato consumption

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.400
was associated with a healthy diet.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.240
If potato consumption was still
associated with diabetes there,

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.320
then that would be concerning.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.080
But here we go...a seventh study,
but this time out of Iran,

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:26.240
where not only is most of their potato
consumption from boiled potatoes,

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.800
but those who eat potatoes had
the healthiest diets and ate

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.160
the most whole plant foods—fruits,
vegetables, whole grains, and beans.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.800
And though the researchers
tried to tease out those other

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.760
dietary factors, those eating
the most boiled potatoes had

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.120
only half the odds of
developing diabetes.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.600
This supports the notion
that it may be hard

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.240
to completely separate
out just the potatoes.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:49.360
The bottom line, this systematic
review concluded, is that

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.080
we really don’t have convincing
evidence to date that the intake

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.280
of potatoes in general is
linked to type 2 diabetes,

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.320
but we should still
probably hold the fries.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.040
In our next story we ask:

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:06.720
do potato eaters live longer or shorter
lives than non-potato eaters?

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.200
Potato intake and the
incidence of hypertension.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.280
Harvard researchers followed
the diets and diseases

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.560
of more than 100,00 men and
women for decades and found that

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.760
those who ate potatoes on most
days—even just baked, boiled,

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.640
or mashed, not just French
fries and potato chips—

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.400
appeared to be at higher risk of
developing high blood pressure.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.200
Okay, but what do people
put on potatoes?

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.720
Salt, not to mention butter; so,
maybe the potatoes are just

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.880
innocent bystanders. Maybe...
but the researchers made attempts

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.360
to tease out the effects
of salt and saturated fat,

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.440
and there still seemed
to be a link between

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.440
potato consumption
and high blood pressure.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.240
Maybe potato eaters are just
meat-and-potatoes people.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:50.880
After all, these same Harvard
researchers have found that

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.400
meat, including poultry alone
appeared to be associated with

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.480
an increased risk of hypertension,

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.760
and the same with even a
moderate amount of canned tuna.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:03.280
So, in the potato study, they were
careful to try to factor out

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:06.800
any effects from the consumption
of all types of animal flesh.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.000
Yet they still found an increased
potato risk and got concerned

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.400
that the association of potato
intake with hypertension

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:14.800
could be a critical
public health problem.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.720
We had assumed
potatoes might actually

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:18.080
decrease high blood pressure

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:20.160
given their high
potassium content,

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:22.800
but they found evidence of
the opposite effect.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.000
Two similar studies performed
in Mediterranean Europe

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.960
did not find any association
between potato consumption

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:30.560
and high blood pressure, though.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.040
Perhaps this is because they
don’t smother their potatoes

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:35.120
in butter and sour cream
in that neck of the woods

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.480
and instead eat potatoes
with other vegetables.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.360
Now, the Harvard folks tried
to control for the bad salty

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:44.160
and fatty dietary components
associated with eating potatoes

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.880
in the West, just like these
researchers tried to factor out

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.320
all the extra vegetables, but
you can’t control for everything.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.120
A primary reason we care
about blood pressure is

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:55.120
because we care
about the consequences.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:57.680
In two studies done in Sweden,
where they primarily eat

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.320
their potatoes boiled, no
evidence was found that

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:02.400
potato consumption was
associated with the risk of

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.840
major cardiovascular disease,
and no relationship was found

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:08.720
between potato consumption
and risk of premature death

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.960
found in Southern Italy either.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.280
In the U.S., however,
potato consumption was

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.360
associated with
increased mortality:

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:18.880
a whopping 65 percent increased
risk of dying from heart disease,

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:21.520
a 26 percent increased
in fatal stroke risk,

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:24.160
a 50 percent increased risk
of dying from cancer,

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:27.120
and increased risk of dying
from all causes put together.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.000
This all disappeared after
adjustment for confounding factors.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.080
In other words, it wasn’t
the potatoes at all.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:36.800
Potato eaters must just
smoke or drink more,

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:38.640
or eat more saturated
fat, or something.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.480
Once you control for
all these other factors,

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.840
the link between potatoes
and death disappears.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.880
This was confirmed in
the NIH-AARP study,

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.840
the largest such study of diet
and health in human history.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.640
If you just separate out the
potatoes, researchers find

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.680
that they are not associated
with increased risk of death,

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.320
with the possible exception
of French fries, which are

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.480
associated with an increased
risk of dying from cancer.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:03.680
Put all the studies
together—20 in all—

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.720
and no significant association
has been found

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.920
between potato consumption
and mortality, though again

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:11.840
fried potatoes
may be the exception.

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:15.520
Even just twice a week, fries
may double one’s risk of

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.400
dying prematurely, independently
of other factors;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:21.680
but the consumption of unfried
potatoes seemed to be neutral.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.280
You know, it’s funny. I’ve
done a bunch of videos on how

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.440
all plant foods are not
created equal, talking about

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.760
healthy vs. unhealthy
plant-based diets.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:32.800
To this end, researchers created
not just an overall

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:35.600
plant-based diet index—just
scoring plant vs. animal foods—

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:37.680
but also a healthy plant-
based diet index (hPDI)

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.760
and an unhealthy plant-
based diet index (uPDI).

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:42.800
The healthy index puts a greater
emphasis on whole plant foods,

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.600
whereas the unhealthy index
scores how much low-quality

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.600
plant foods you’re eating,
grouping potatoes

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:49.600
along with soda, and cake,
and Wonder Bread.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.000
Then, when you run the numbers,
the more plant-based you eat

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.280
the longer you live, the lower
your risk of cardiovascular disease.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:08:59.520
In other words, more plant foods
and less animal foods are

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:03.040
associated with a significantly
lower risk of dying prematurely.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.800
This benefit was limited,
though, to those eating the

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:10.000
healthier plant food diets,
but they were surprised that

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.960
those eating all that
processed plant-based crap

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.360
didn’t live significantly
shorter lives.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:18.400
Now maybe, that’s just because
they were eating fewer

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:21.120
animal products, and that’s
really the primary determinant

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.200
of lifespan here, or maybe the
lack of an association between

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.440
less healthy plant-based diets
and mortality outcomes

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:30.480
is because potatoes were kind
of coming to the rescue.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.600
And indeed, higher intake of
potatoes did appear protective;

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.360
so, given these conﬂicting ﬁndings,
future studies may consider

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:40.480
just resigning fried potatoes
to the unhealthy list.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:44.720
Now, in terms of mortality, fried
potatoes may not be as bad

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:47.760
as fried meat—fried
chicken and fried fish—

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.320
but that’s not
really saying much.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:52.960
The French fry death data
gave the industry trade group

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:57.120
Potatoes USA a bit of a
chip on their shoulder,

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:00.240
reminding readers that observational
studies can only prove

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.320
correlation, not causation,
to which the authors replied,

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:06.960
“our data add to the pressing
public health calls

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.120
to limit fried
potato consumption.”

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:14.000
French fries may be so bad for
you that it wouldn’t be ethical

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.840
to do an interventional study and
randomize people to eat them.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:22.320
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.600
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:28.160
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.920
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:35.600
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:38.880
